<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kelantan Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1762 CE – present (approx.) · capital Kota Bharu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">North-east Malay sultanate, long a tributary of Siam, now a state of Malaysia known for its strong Malay and Islamic identity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kelantan had been ruled by various local princes for centuries, often under the influence of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pattani-kingdom">Pattani</a> and Siam. In 1762 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-yunus">Long Yunus</a>, of a Pattani-linked family, unified the territory and founded the line of rulers that still holds the throne, and in the early 19th century his successors adopted the title of sultan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kelantan was a populous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing state on the delta of the Kelantan river, with fishing, weaving and metalwork. Its rulers sent the bunga mas to Siam while keeping internal autonomy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ii-of-kelantan">Muhammad II</a> strengthened royal power from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-bharu">Kota Bharu</a> in the mid 19th century. Wayang kulit, kite flying and Islamic schools were prominent.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-siamese-treaty-of-1909">Anglo-Siamese Treaty of 1909</a> placed Kelantan under British protection as one of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/unfederated-malay-states">Unfederated Malay States</a>. After Japanese and Thai occupation it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-malaya">Federation of Malaya</a>, and today it is a state of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia</a> whose sultan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-v-of-kelantan">Muhammad V</a>, served as king from 2016 to 2019.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kelantan-sultanate.jpg" alt="Kelantan Sultanate" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Aimanrasul · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Standard_of_the_Sultan_of_Kelantan_(Sultan_Muhammad_V).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1762 CE – present (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kota Bharu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Long Yunus, Muhammad II, Muhammad V</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kelantan Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Kelantan, Kelantan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1894&amp;focus=kelantan-sultanate" aria-label="Kelantan Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7513 1948 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kelantan Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7833 2204L7754 1922M7833 2204L7823 2316M7833 2204L7823 2316"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7754 1922h0M7823 2316h0M7823 2316h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7833 2204h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7833 2204h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kota Bharu. <a href="/atlas?year=1894&amp;focus=kelantan-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

